    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d234">

                <pb facs="0402" n="303" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg303"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">362.1121 Simpson, Lawrence/351: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Dodd</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-05T18:00:00-05:00">November 5, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. Referring to your 323 of November 4, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="22" xml:id="d234-f1"
                    /> you are requested to support Simpson’s petition for pardon by oral
                    representations to <pb facs="0403" n="304" xml:id="pg304"/> the Foreign Office.
                    It is suggested that in doing so you urge upon the Foreign Office (1) the fact
                    that Simpson’s petition indicates that he is penitent and that he has promised
                    never to interfere in German political affairs again; (2) the fact that Simpson
                    has already served a substantial part of his term; and (3) the possibility that
                    continued imprisonment seems likely further to impair his physical condition.
                    You may of course present any additional considerations which commend themselves
                    to your judgment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
